{
  "explainer": {
    "title": "The base rate fallacy: a 3D surface of Bayes' theorem",
    "explanation": "The surface shows $P(\\text{sick}\\mid +)$ for every combination of prevalence and specificity [S5]. Move the prevalence and notice: when the disease is rare, most positive results are false positives, even when the test is very sensitive [S4].",
    "html": "<script>\nconst ui = Viz.app({ title: \"The base rate fallacy\", subtitle: \"How much is a positive test really worth? It depends a lot on how common the disease is.\", columns: 2 });\nconst s3 = Viz.scene3d(ui.card(\"P(sick | positive) as a function of prevalence and specificity\", 400), {\n  camera: [7.2, -4.2, 4.6], target: [1.5, 1.5, 0.9], axisLabels: [\"Prevalence\", \"Specificity\", \"P\"], axes: 3.2,\n});\nconst p2 = Viz.plot2d(ui.card(\"Slice: the current specificity\", 400), { x: [-3, -0.3], y: [0, 1], height: 340, xLabel: \"log₁₀ prevalence\", yLabel: \"P(sick | +)\" });\n\nlet prev = 0.01, sens = 0.95, spec = 0.95;\nconst post = (pi, se, sp) => (se * pi) / (se * pi + (1 - sp) * (1 - pi));\nconst specOf = (y) => 0.8 + (0.199 * y) / 3;     // y ∈ [0,3] ↦ specificity ∈ [0.80, 0.999]\nconst yOf = (sp) => ((sp - 0.8) * 3) / 0.199;\nconst pct = (v) => (100 * v).toFixed(1) + \"%\";\n\nconst eq = ui.formula(\"\");\nconst out = ui.readouts({});\n\nfunction update() {\n  s3.clear();\n  // x' = log10(prevalence) + 3  ∈ [0, 2.7]\n  s3.surface((x, y) => 2.5 * post(10 ** (x - 3), sens, specOf(y)), { x: [0, 2.7], y: [0, 3], segments: 70, opacity: 0.92 });\n  const x = Math.log10(prev) + 3, y = yOf(spec), z = 2.5 * post(prev, sens, spec);\n  s3.line([[x, y, 0], [x, y, z]], { color: \"#0f172a\", radius: 0.015 });\n  s3.sphere([x, y, z], { r: 0.11, color: \"#dc2626\" });\n  s3.label(pct(z / 2.5), [x, y, z + 0.35], { color: \"#dc2626\" });\n\n  p2.draw((g) => {\n    g.fn((lx) => post(10 ** lx, sens, spec), { label: \"Current specificity\", fill: true });\n    g.fn((lx) => post(10 ** lx, sens, 0.999), { label: \"Specificity 99.9%\", dash: [6, 4], width: 2 });\n    g.vline(Math.log10(prev));\n    g.points([[Math.log10(prev), post(prev, sens, spec)]], { r: 6, color: \"#dc2626\" });\n  });\n\n  const N = 10000, sick = prev * N, tp = sens * sick, fp = (1 - spec) * (N - sick);\n  out.set({\n    \"Sick, out of 10,000\": Math.round(sick),\n    \"True positives\": Math.round(tp),\n    \"False positives\": Math.round(fp),\n    \"$P(\\\\text{sick}\\\\mid +)$\": pct(post(prev, sens, spec)),\n  });\n  eq.set(`P(\\\\text{sick}\\\\mid +)=\\\\frac{${sens.toFixed(2)}\\\\cdot ${prev.toFixed(3)}}{${sens.toFixed(2)}\\\\cdot ${prev.toFixed(3)}+${(1 - spec).toFixed(3)}\\\\cdot ${(1 - prev).toFixed(3)}}=${post(prev, sens, spec).toFixed(3)}`);\n}\n\nui.slider({ label: \"Disease prevalence\", min: -3, max: -0.3, step: 0.01, value: Math.log10(prev), format: (v) => pct(10 ** v) }, (v) => { prev = 10 ** v; update(); });\nui.slider({ label: \"Sensitivity  P(+ | sick)\", min: 0.5, max: 0.999, step: 0.001, value: sens, format: pct }, (v) => { sens = v; update(); });\nui.slider({ label: \"Specificity  P(− | healthy)\", min: 0.8, max: 0.999, step: 0.001, value: spec, format: pct }, (v) => { spec = v; update(); });\nui.note(\"<b>What to notice:</b> the surface is almost flat and close to zero when the disease is rare, even when the test is excellent. Most positives are false positives because there are many more healthy people. <b>Try:</b> prevalence 0.1% and specificity 95%, then raise the specificity to 99.9%. What happens to $P(\\\\text{sick}\\\\mid +)$?\");\nupdate();\n</script>\n",
    "concept": "Bayes' theorem",
    "citations": [
      "S5",
      "S4"
    ]
  }
}
